## 2. Source of truth

Before writing any code, the implementer (Copilot or human) must read the actual OpenHands source — not summaries, not this playbook's descriptions — from the cloned `software-agent-sdk` repository already present on the office laptop/repo mirror:

- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/event/` — event model
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/agent/` — agent loop
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/llm/` — LLM abstraction, `RouterLLM`
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/conversation/` — `Conversation`, `ConversationState`, local/remote impls
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/tool/` — `ToolDefinition`, `Executor`, Action/Observation base
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/security/` — `SecurityAnalyzerBase`, confirmation policies
- `openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/context/` or condenser module — `CondenserBase`
- `openhands-tools/openhands/tools/` — `TerminalTool`, `FileEditorTool`, `TaskTrackerTool`, `BrowserTool`, MCP tool support
- `openhands-workspace/` — `LocalWorkspace`, `RemoteWorkspace`
- `openhands-agent-server/` — REST/WebSocket server implementation, persistence model

Every Agent Spark module must cite, in its module docstring, which OpenHands source file it is a port of (e.g., `"""Port of openhands-sdk/openhands/sdk/event/base.py. See ADR-0001."""`). This creates an auditable trail for parity review and for a future migration back to the real SDK if it is ever approved for direct use.

