## 3. Feature parity checklist (acceptance criteria)

Every item below must exist in Agent Spark with an equivalent, testable implementation. This is the literal Definition of Done — a PR that doesn't map to every applicable row is incomplete.

### 3.1 Core agent loop
- [ ] `SparkAgent` — stateless reasoning-action loop: condense (if needed) → call LLM → parse tool calls → risk-check → execute → append events
- [ ] `SparkConversation` factory — returns local or remote conversation implementation behind one symmetric API
- [ ] Event-sourced state: immutable `SparkEvent` base + `MessageEvent`, `ActionEvent`, `ObservationEvent`, `SystemPromptEvent` subclasses, appended to an `EventLog`
- [ ] Multi-agent delegation — one `SparkAgent` can hand a sub-task to another `SparkAgent` and receive its result back into the parent's event log

### 3.2 LLM layer
- [ ] `SparkLLM` — provider-agnostic wrapper; must work against **Tachyon** via `base_url` + `api_key`, with no Codemind-specific branching inside `agentspark-core` (Tachyon wiring lives only in the integration adapter, Section 5.2)
- [ ] `SparkRouterLLM` — route different steps/tasks to different underlying models
- [ ] Retries, prompt caching, cost/usage tracking (`SparkConversationStats`)
- [ ] Pluggable credential store interface (reads from `.env` by default)

### 3.3 Tools
- [ ] `TerminalTool` — persistent shell session, sandbox-aware
- [ ] `FileEditorTool` — structured view/create/edit (not raw diff patching)
- [ ] `TaskTrackerTool` — todo-list style task planning/tracking
- [ ] `BrowserTool` — web browsing/interaction (implement even if unused initially — parity, not utility, is the requirement)
- [ ] MCP tool consumption — Agent Spark can call any external MCP server as a tool source
- [ ] `ToolDefinition` + `Executor` base classes — typed Action/Observation schemas
- [ ] Dual export: every `ToolDefinition` produces both an OpenAI function-calling schema (`to_openai_tool()`) and an MCP tool schema (`to_mcp_tool()`) from one definition

### 3.4 Safety / control
- [ ] `SparkSecurityAnalyzerBase` — pluggable per-action risk scoring interface
- [ ] Confirmation policies: `AlwaysConfirm`, `ConfirmRisky`, `NeverConfirm`
- [ ] Human-in-the-loop pause/approve/reject on flagged actions, surfaced through whatever notification/UI channel Codemind already uses (no new channel invented)

### 3.5 Context management
- [ ] `SparkCondenserBase` — pluggable context compression when history nears context window
- [ ] At least one concrete condenser implementation, wired to the integration adapter (Section 5.2) rather than hardcoded into core

### 3.6 Execution environments
- [ ] `LocalWorkspace` — direct host filesystem execution
- [ ] `RemoteWorkspace` — talks to a `SparkAgentServer` over REST/WebSocket
- [ ] Sandboxed remote execution (container- or pod-per-task) — see Section 6 if OCP-specific provisioning requires infra outside current Codemind dependencies

### 3.7 Server / remote
- [ ] `SparkAgentServer` — REST + WebSocket API for remote conversation execution
- [ ] Persistence directory / durable store for resumable conversations across restarts
- [ ] Support for scaling execution across multiple concurrent sandboxed workspaces

### 3.8 Extensibility
- [ ] `SparkAgentContext` (skills) — reusable domain-specific instructions injected into agent context
- [ ] Plugin system — pluggable skill/tool/hook sources, with a defined merge strategy (skills override by name, tool configs merge by key, hooks concatenate) — mirroring OpenHands' Plugin 1.0 semantics
- [ ] Lifecycle hooks around steps/actions/observations

### 3.9 Cross-cutting
- [ ] Fully typed (Pydantic) config and state throughout — no untyped dicts as public API surface
- [ ] No telemetry, no phone-home, no network call not explicitly configured by the caller
- [ ] Unit tests for every public class listed above
- [ ] Module-level docstrings citing the OpenHands source file being ported (Section 2)

**Explicitly out of scope for this phase** (do not build unless separately requested): `agentspark-cli` (terminal UI), an issue-resolver equivalent, and OpenHands Cloud–style hosted credits. Building these without being asked is itself a violation of Rule 1 ("no invention").

